M23 RED-PROOF — the publish flip locks every sources row for the length of its transaction
date: 2026-08-01T22:39:33Z   pg: PostgreSQL 14.19 (throwaway, ephemeral)

THE FINDING (deep audit M23). publish-flip.mjs:151 is a bare BEGIN, so the transaction runs at
READ COMMITTED and every statement takes a new snapshot. The legality gate asks 'is the
PUBLISHED corpus legal' over the whole table; barnes-notes is deliberately NOT flipped, so
nothing in the transaction touched it and a concurrent session could publish it between the
gate's read and COMMIT. The gate would have passed over a corpus that is not the one committed.

THE FIX. The pre-snapshot SELECT becomes ... FOR UPDATE, plus SET LOCAL lock_timeout='15s'.
Raising the isolation level was rejected: under REPEATABLE READ the before/after delta check
compares one snapshot to itself and becomes a check that CANNOT FAIL.

SESSION A holds the flip's lock. SESSION B attempts exactly the concurrent publish the audit
describes, on exactly the row it names:

  A: BEGIN; SELECT slug,status FROM sources ORDER BY slug FOR UPDATE; pg_sleep(4); COMMIT;
  B: BEGIN; SET LOCAL lock_timeout='1s'; UPDATE sources SET status='published' WHERE slug='barnes-notes'; COMMIT;

  B ->  ERROR:  canceling statement due to lock timeout
        CONTEXT:  while updating tuple (0,1) in relation "sources"

  final state:  barnes-notes | staged      <- B did NOT publish
                john-gill    | published

Before the fix the same B commits immediately and the flip's gate never sees it.

COMPOSES WITH THE RE-INGEST GUARD (M21/M22, same day). src/ingest/reingest-guard.ts takes
FOR UPDATE on the sources row it is about to delete sections for, so a re-ingest and a flip
now mutually exclude. That is what makes the flip's badSections leg trustworthy without
locking 72,863 section rows.
